    Section(
      title: '+ Interpret the Waddell signs. Differentiate symptom magnification from malingering. What other assessment tools can help determine effort and consistency?',
      content:
          '**Waddell Signs — Proper Interpretation**:\n\nWaddell signs were originally described as **behavioral indicators of illness behavior**, NOT as proof of malingering or faking. They indicate that psychological and social factors are contributing to the pain presentation. However, 5/5 positive Waddell categories, combined with significant inconsistency between observed function and reported limitations, raises serious concern.\n\n**Critical Distinction**:\n- **Symptom magnification**: Conscious or unconscious amplification of real symptoms. May be driven by fear-avoidance, catastrophizing, secondary gain (financial), or psychological distress. The patient may have genuine pain that is being amplified.\n- **Malingering**: Deliberate fabrication or exaggeration of symptoms for external gain (financial, legal). DSM-5 lists it as a "condition" to be considered when medicolegal context is present, symptoms are inconsistent with objective findings, and there is clear external incentive.\n- **Factitious disorder**: Fabrication of symptoms for the "sick role" without external gain (NOT applicable here given financial incentive).\n\n**This patient demonstrates**: Non-organic signs on exam (5/5 Waddell), significant inconsistency between observed and reported function (surveillance video vs. stated limitations, seated SLR vs. supine SLR), and clear external incentive (workers\' comp, pending lawsuit). This constellation raises concern for **symptom magnification with possible secondary gain motivation**, though definitive determination of malingering requires careful documentation and is ultimately a legal, not medical, determination.\n\n**Additional Assessment Tools**:\n1. **Functional Capacity Evaluation (FCE)**: Standardized, multi-day assessment of physical capabilities with built-in consistency checks. Identifies submaximal effort through coefficient of variation analysis.\n2. **Grip strength testing**: Rapid exchange grip test (alternating dynamometer positions) — inconsistent results suggest submaximal effort.\n3. **Symptom Validity Tests**: For cognitive complaints — TOMM (Test of Memory Malingering), Word Memory Test.\n4. **Questionnaires**: Pain Disability Index, Oswestry Disability Index — compare self-report to observed function.\n5. **Coefficient of Variation (CV)**: In FCE testing, a CV >15% across repeated trials of the same task suggests inconsistent effort.\n\n**Important Caveats**:\n- Waddell signs have limited sensitivity and specificity for malingering\n- Presence of non-organic signs does NOT mean absence of organic pathology — he DOES have a documented disc herniation\n- Psychological factors (depression, anxiety, catastrophizing) can produce non-organic signs without conscious intent to deceive\n- The physiatrist\'s role is to document objective findings and inconsistencies — NOT to make a legal determination of fraud',
