          '**Ethical and Legal Obligations of the IME Physician**:\n\n1. **No physician-patient relationship**: The IME does NOT create a treating physician-patient relationship. The examiner\'s obligation is to provide an **objective, evidence-based medical opinion** — not to advocate for the examinee OR the insurer.\n\n2. **Objectivity is paramount**: You are NOT the insurance company\'s doctor, despite being retained by them. You must resist pressure from either side. The phone call from the adjuster requesting that you "state clearly that this guy is faking" is inappropriate and must be handled firmly.\n\n**Response to the adjuster**: \'I appreciate you sharing the surveillance information, which I have reviewed and will reference in my report. However, my role as an IME physician is to provide an independent, objective medical opinion based on all available evidence. I will document the inconsistencies I observed and let the medical findings speak for themselves. I cannot and will not advocate for a particular outcome. If you have specific medical questions you would like me to address, please submit them in writing.\'\n\n3. **Report Writing Principles**:\n- **Document observations objectively**: "On supine SLR testing, the examinee reported severe pain at 30 degrees. During seated examination, the knee was passively extended to 80 degrees without pain complaint, representing a positive distraction test."\n- **Avoid conclusory language**: Do NOT write "the examinee is malingering" or "faking." Instead: "The examination demonstrated significant inconsistency between observed functional capacity and reported limitations, with 5 of 5 Waddell categories positive for non-organic signs."\n- **Reference surveillance objectively**: "Review of surveillance video dated [date] demonstrates the examinee performing activities including lifting objects estimated at 15-20 lbs and operating a push lawn mower for approximately 30 minutes, which is inconsistent with his reported inability to lift more than 5 lbs or stand for more than 5 minutes."\n- **Separate opinions from facts**: Clearly delineate what is objective finding versus clinical opinion.\n- **Answer the specific questions asked**: MMI determination, impairment rating, causation, work capacity.\n\n4. **Obligations to the Examinee**:\n- Inform the examinee at the start of the exam that no physician-patient relationship exists and that the findings will be reported to the requesting party\n- Conduct a thorough, respectful examination\n- If a potentially dangerous medical condition is discovered during the exam, you have an ethical duty to inform the examinee (e.g., if you found signs of cauda equina syndrome)\n\n5. **Record Keeping**: Maintain your own copy of the report and all reviewed records. IME reports are discoverable in litigation and may be subject to cross-examination. Every statement must be defensible.',
